# Every program's emitted C, blessed — the instrument that survives the
# bootstrap.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. heroes-cli/tests/differential.rs in the Rust bootstrap at `m-bootstrap-archive` asks the one
# question no golden can ask: do the TWO compilers emit the same C? Its
# oracle is a second implementation, so it is the only test in this
# repository that must go dark at the archive — and the day it was written
# it found 101 of 127 programs disagreeing, in three classes, every one of
# them invisible to every other instrument (panel 085 R6). Archiving the
# bootstrap without a successor would archive the question with it.
#
# WHAT REPLACES THE ORACLE. The bytes under `tests/emission/`, one file
# per program, written while both compilers still existed and the
# differential was green over all of them. A blessed file is therefore not
# one compiler's opinion: it is what two independent implementations
# agreed on, byte for byte. This suite asks whether today's compiler still
# says the same thing.
#
# WHAT IT CATCHES THAT NOTHING ELSE DOES. Both classes the differential
# found are silent everywhere else. A float literal spelled in hex instead
# of decimal is the same number, so `run/` prints the same output and
# `corpus` behaves identically; a `#line` restore naming a file nothing
# creates compiles clean, so `warnings` stays green. The difference is in
# the bytes alone, and only something that stores the bytes can see it.
#
# WHY THE WHOLE C AND NOT A HASH. Measured on 2026-08-18: 142 programs,
# 5.4 MB, 162,878 lines of C. A manifest of 142 hashes detects the same
# changes for 142 lines — and cannot say WHAT changed, because once the
# bootstrap is gone nothing can reconstruct the previous emission except an
# old checkout and a rebuild. design.md §4.17's rule for a diagnostic is
# the rule for a failure here: everything needed to act, without opening
# another file. `seed/heroes.c` already pays this price at 21 MB for one
# program, and it pays it for the same reason.
#
# BLESSING IS DELIBERATE, and it is typed:
#
#     UPDATE_EMISSION=1 heroes run tests/harness/main.hero -- <compiler> emission
#
# or for the programs whose label holds a third word alone, `emission
# run/loop`, which is how a program with nothing blessed is told to be
# blessed (defect 2341): what is not named is neither compared nor written.
#
# CLAUDE.md §9 follows the bytes: a regeneration that turns red green has
# its diff read and quoted in the commit body. This is the one suite in the
# harness that writes into the repository, which is why the guards below
# are not decoration.
#
# THE FOUR GUARDS, each one a way an oracle stops being one:
#   - the FLOOR: fewer blessed emissions than the last measured count is a
#     red harness, never a smaller green one, and so are fewer refusals, the
#     programs with nothing blessed, counted apart (defect 341);
#   - COLLISION: two programs mapping to one blessed file would leave one
#     of them uncompared and say nothing;
#   - ORPHANS: a blessed file no program claims is a program renamed or
#     deleted, and the bytes left behind are checked by nobody;
#   - the SKIP FLOOR: a machine missing every C library says so instead of
#     reporting green, as every other suite here does.

use absence
use cases
use floors
use placed_trees
use platform_skips
use report
use shell
use strings
use suite_descriptors

constant STORE: str
    "tests/emission"

# The golden directories whose cases reach the emitter. `check/` and
# `unsupported/` are deliberately absent and it is not laziness: those
# programs are refused before the emitter runs, so they have no C at all,
# and their agreement is what the `check` and `unsupported` suites pin.
#
# `fixedbugs/` IS here, and the reason is worth knowing: those cases are
# wrong FFI bindings, and their C is exactly where the two compilers once
# disagreed on a fixed array. They carry no `.expected`, which is why this
# suite asks `cases.programs`, the walk `cases.collect` lays the pair rule on,
# and not `cases.collect`: the pairing invariant belongs to the suites whose
# artifact is a snapshot. Until defect 2341 it read each directory itself and
# took its `.hero` files, so a case that is a folder had no blessed emission.
#
# **`--emit-c` asks clang before it writes a byte, so most of them have no C**
# (defect 298). Its artifact is that of a build that passed: the pointee check
# runs on its path since 2026-09-16 (defect 048, panel 157), because four of
# these cases emitted C that compiled clean and wrote over the adjacent stack
# slot (design.md §1.12), and since 2026-09-17 the build's rounds compile every
# unit with clang before the C is written, and do not link (defect 049,
# `selfhost/cli/produce.hero`). So a binding clang refuses is refused at exit
# 1 with no C, 30 of the 38 measured on 2026-10-05, and the loop below passes
# such a program **on the condition that nothing is blessed for it**: retiring
# its file is what records the decision. The 8 that emit are bindings clang
# compiles, `ffi-missing-link` among them, which only a link refuses. What
# this suite compares is the C of every program whose units clang compiles.
#
# Until defect 298 (2026-10-05) this comment said `--emit-c` never calls
# clang, so all fourteen emit, which was measured when written and false from
# 2026-09-16; then that the round's tag half discards clang's verdict, false
# from 2026-09-17. The suite's verdicts never rested on either: a refused
# program with nothing blessed passes by defect 048's rule.
constant GROUPS: [str]
    [
        "emit"
        "fixedbugs"
        "ir"
        "run"
    ]

constant EXAMPLES: str
    "examples"

# The two names an example's entry point takes: `main.hero`, and the
# single-file form `whole.hero` that `examples/calculator/` carries.
constant ENTRIES: [str]
    [
        "main"
        "whole"
    ]

# The floor is the real count rather than a round number below it, for the reason
# `suite_golden.hero` gives: slack in a floor is a case that may stop running.
#
# **142 on 2026-08-18** — 127 golden cases (emit 6 · fixedbugs 14 · ir 20 · run
# 87) and 15 example programs. **225 on 2026-09-12**, so the slack that sentence
# forbids had reached 83, and the sentence went on reading as true the whole
# time. That is the class `.claude/rules/module-shape.md` names: a premise about
# the world expires in silence while the argument for it stays valid. Re-based by
# `floors.check`, which is the caller the rule never had.
#
# **233 on 2026-09-14** — emit 6 · fixedbugs 30 · ir 23 · run 119, and 55 example
# programs. The slack had reached 8 in two days, which is the rate this floor
# actually drifts at and the reason it is re-based at a close rather than when
# somebody notices. Found while repairing what the full net caught before the
# push: three of the eight are that repair's own cases.
#
# **295 on 2026-09-25** — emit 7 · fixedbugs 38 · ir 23 · run 172, and 55
# example programs. Raised at panel 177's item 3 landing because the seven
# `run` cases its check-time rule owes for the shapes it must leave alive took
# the count past the 25 % slack `floors.check` allows, and the suite said so.
#
# **304 on 2026-09-25**: emit 7 · fixedbugs 38 · ir 23 · run 181, and 55 example
# programs (`ls tests/golden/<group>/*.hero | wc -l`, and `ls -d examples/*/`),
# re-based at panel 177's landing, whose fifteen `run/dead-*` cases and two
# `run/limit-*` cases carried the count past the 25% margin the check allows:
# 288 passed under a floor of 233 the day before, so the slack had reached 55
# in eleven days.
#
# **296 on 2026-09-25**, printed by this suite when defect 097's eight run cases
# took the count more than 25% past 233.
#
# **326 on 2026-09-25, at the merge of step 14's two halves with step 15** —
# emit 7 · fixedbugs 38 · ir 23 · run 203, and 55 example programs: the seven
# cases of the checker's half, the seventeen of the runtime's and the eight of
# defect 097, counted in one tree.
#
# **408 on 2026-10-05**, printed by this suite at defect 245's repair (panel
# 192's R1), whose thirteen `run/fixedbugs-245-*` cases took the count more
# than 25% past 326: emit 7 · fixedbugs 38 · ir 25 · run 283, and 55 example
# programs, by the commands above. The slack had reached 69 in ten days.
#
# **IT COUNTS THE EMISSIONS BLESSED SINCE DEFECT 341** (2026-10-07). Every
# number above counted the programs collected, and a program `--emit-c`
# refuses, with nothing blessed, passes here with no C compared: 35 of the 502
# collected that day, 32 of the 40 in `fixedbugs/` and three in `ir/`, so the
# floor could not see blessed files retired along with programs that came to
# be refused, which is an oracle stopping comparing in silence. So the floor
# is the programs whose emission is blessed, a count every machine reads the
# same: one it cannot emit for a library it lacks is skipped by name, and
# floored below a third. **467 on 2026-10-07**: `ls tests/emission/*.c | wc
# -l`, every one claimed (the suite's `orphans`), and the suite's own count.
# **589 on 2026-10-10**, at batch 19's gate, the suite's own count.
# **737 on 2026-11-06**, at M-core-packages' first batch gate, the suite's own
# count: the batch's one new program, `examples/packages/`, took the trunk's 736
# past 589 by more than a quarter. **752 on 2026-11-07**, at batch 23's gate,
# the count its blessing run told as `FLOOR emission`; the two met at batch 1's
# merge of the trunk, which keeps the higher.
constant LEAST: i64
    752

# **The refusals, counted apart** (defect 341): the programs with nothing
# blessed, each of which passes only as a refusal, exit 1 and no C. Floored so
# that a walk that stops reaching them, or cases deleted with them, is a red
# and not a smaller green, as the blessed floor above does for the rest. **35
# on 2026-10-07**, `fixedbugs/` 32 and `ir/` 3, the collected count less the
# blessed one. **42 at the round's close in lane land-214, 2026-10-14**, the
# count the gate read alone: a blessing run had told `FLOOR emission` at 45,
# three new `run` cases counted before their emissions were blessed.
constant LEAST_REFUSED: i64
    42

# One program and the bytes it was blessed to emit.
record Program
    source: str
    stored: str
    label: str

# `only` narrows the suite to the programs whose label holds it, `run/loop` or a
# part of one (defect 2341): the gate of one repair, and the blessing of one new
# program. A narrowed run is not the whole suite, so it asks none of the whole
# suite's questions, its floors and its orphans, and `main` says so on its line.
function run_all(scratch: str, compiler: str, only: str) -> report.Report?
    programs = collect(scratch)?
    r: report.Report @= report.empty()

    # **The floor is checked from both sides HERE and not inside `sweep`**, and
    # the seam is deliberate: `sweep` takes its floor as a parameter and the
    # cases below hand it 1 or 2 over a two-file tree, so a decay check in there
    # would call every test's stub tree outgrown. This is where the REAL floor
    # meets the REAL count, and the refusals meet theirs.
    if only == ""
        blessed = blessed_among(programs)
        floors.check(@r, label: "emission", found: blessed, floor: LEAST, what: "programs whose emission is blessed")
        floors.check(
            @r,
            label: "emission",
            found: programs.len() - blessed,
            floor: LEAST_REFUSED,
            what: "programs with nothing blessed, each one `--emit-c` must refuse"
        )
    swept = sweep(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, store: STORE, programs: programs, update: blessing(), least: LEAST, only: only)?

    # Every blessed emission compiled, after the sweep and apart from it, so
    # the sweep's own tests, which run it over stub trees, judge bytes alone.
    # An emission that names Darwin's own C is compiled where it was blessed
    # and skipped elsewhere by name, as its bytes are: the CI's first run
    # over this pass (40756360364, 2026-10-28) refused six on both Linux legs
    # (`pthread_mutex_t`'s `__sig`, `re_pattern_buffer`'s `re_magic`,
    # `passwd`'s `pw_change`, `SDL_MouseWheelEvent`'s `integer_x`) and one on
    # Windows (`tm`'s `tm_gmtoff`), every one a name of `darwin_emissions`.
    for one in narrowed(programs: programs, only: only)
        if shell.exists(one.stored) && !blessing()
            elsewhere = platform_skips.emission_reason(one.label)

            if elsewhere.is_err()
                compiles(@r, scratch: scratch, one: one)
            else
                report.keep_skip(@r, label: "emission/" + one.label + "/compiles", detail: "  " + elsewhere.must())

    return ok(report.tally(one: r, two: swept))

# How many of `programs` have a blessed emission: the ones this suite compares,
# or skips by name where the machine lacks a library they bind. A program with
# nothing blessed passes only as a refusal, so it is no emission compared and
# is counted apart (defect 341).
function blessed_among(programs: [Program]) -> i64
    count: i64 @= 0

    for one in programs
        if shell.exists(one.stored)
            count @ count + 1

    return count

# Whether this run blesses: `UPDATE_EMISSION=1` and nothing else. A value that
# is not UTF-8 is not `1`, so it blesses nothing and the run compares, which
# writes nothing; that is the value's own answer, not a default taken for an
# unread one (defect 243, `shell.env`).
function blessing() -> bool
    return match shell.env(name: "UPDATE_EMISSION")
        .ok value => value == "1"
        .err _    => false

# Every program in the repository that reaches the emitter, with the path
# its blessed emission lives at.
function collect(scratch: str) -> [Program]?
    return collected(scratch: scratch, root: "")

# The programs of the tree at `root`, "" for the repository or a folder of
# the tests' own ending in `/`.
#
# A group's programs are `cases.programs`', the one walk of a golden
# directory: each `.hero` file, and each folder `<name>.case/` as ONE program,
# its `main.hero` (defect 2341). Its source is that file's path from the
# repository's root, which `sweep` builds as the `run` form builds every case
# (`suite_run.one_case`), so the compiler reads the catalogue and the placed
# trees beside it, as it does for every build. A folder not named `.case` is
# a case's modules, no program of its own.
#
# The stored name is the program's own path flattened, `run/loop.hero`
# becoming `run-loop.c` and `run/pkg.case/main.hero`, a case's name being its
# folder's less `.case`, `run-pkg.c`: one flat directory rather than a
# mirrored tree, because it is one `find` and one listing to check for
# orphans. Flattening can collide, `run/pkg.hero` beside `run/pkg.case/`, so
# the sweep asks; it does not assume.
function collected(scratch: str, root: str) -> [Program]?
    found: [Program] @= []

    for group in GROUPS
        for item in cases.programs(scratch: scratch, dir: root + "tests/golden/" + group)?
            found @ found.push(
                Program(source: item.source, stored: root + STORE + "/" + group + "-" + item.name + ".c", label: group + "/" + item.name)
            )

    for dir in shell.list_directories(scratch: scratch, dir: root + EXAMPLES)?
        base = strings.base_name(path: dir)

        for entry in ENTRIES
            source = dir + "/" + entry + ".hero"

            if !read_file(source).is_err()
                found @ found.push(
                    Program(source: source, stored: root + STORE + "/examples-" + base + "-" + entry + ".c", label: "examples/" + base + "/" + entry)
                )

    return ok(found)

# The programs whose label holds `only`, every one where it is empty: the
# third word, as `cases.matching` reads it of a case's name.
function narrowed(programs: [Program], only: str) -> [Program]
    if only == ""
        return programs
    kept: [Program] @= []

    for one in programs
        if strings.contains(text: one.label, needle: only)
            kept @ kept.push(one)

    return kept

# The sweep itself, with the store, the program list, the floor and the third
# word as parameters, so the tests at the bottom can run it over a tree of
# their own instead of over the repository.
#
# A narrowed sweep walks the programs `only` names and asks no floor and no
# orphan, the whole suite's questions (defect 2341): every blessed file of a
# program it does not walk would read as an orphan, and its count as short.
function sweep(scratch: str, compiler: str, store: str, programs: [Program], update: bool, least: i64, only: str) -> report.Report?
    r: report.Report @= report.empty()
    blessed = blessed_among(programs)

    if only == "" && blessed < least
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "emission",
            detail: "  " + blessed.to_str() + " programs whose emission is blessed, and there were " + least.to_str() + " — an oracle that quietly stops comparing is worse than no oracle"
        )
    seen: {str: str} @= {}
    skipped: i64 @= 0
    walked = narrowed(programs: programs, only: only)

    for one in walked
        earlier = seen[one.stored]

        if !earlier.is_err()
            report.keep_failure(
                @r,
                label: "emission/" + one.label,
                detail: "  " + one.stored + " is claimed by " + earlier.must() + " as well — two programs, one blessed file, and one of the two would stop being compared"
            )
            continue
        seen[one.stored] @ one.label

        # An emission that names this platform's own C is compared where it
        # was blessed and skipped here by name, its file seen, so no orphan
        # (`platform_skips.emission_reason`, defect 2111).
        elsewhere = platform_skips.emission_reason(one.label)

        if !elsewhere.is_err()
            skipped @ skipped + 1
            report.keep_skip(@r, label: "emission/" + one.label, detail: "  " + elsewhere.must())
            continue
        said = shell.run_guarded(
            scratch: scratch,
            words: [compiler, "build", one.source, "--emit-c"]
        )

        # The refusal's own words (defect 317): a compiler that wrote bytes
        # that are not UTF-8 ran, and its emission is refused as such.
        if said.is_err()
            report.keep_failure(
                @r,
                label: "emission/" + one.label,
                detail: "  " + one.source + ": " + shell.refusal(said)
            )
            continue

        # THE EXIT CODE FIRST, because a divergence there is the louder
        # finding — the differential's own lesson: three programs got C
        # from the port that does not compile, and comparing only the bytes
        # would have reported "one file missing" and buried the reason. A
        # program whose units clang compiles emits; one clang refuses exits 1
        # with no C, as 30 of the 38 in `fixedbugs/` do (`GROUPS` says why).
        #
        # A program this machine cannot emit for a library it lacks is skipped,
        # by name, and floored below. It is the machine's only where the
        # program binds what the compiler says is missing and the machine,
        # asked, lacks it (`absence`); until defect 246 the compiler's words
        # alone made the skip.
        if said.must().code != 0
            lacks = absence.machine_lacks(scratch: scratch, path: one.source, expected: "", said: said.must().err)

            if !lacks.is_err()
                skipped @ skipped + 1
                report.keep_skip(@r, label: "emission/" + one.label, detail: "  " + lacks.must())
            else if said.must().code == 1 && read_file(one.stored).is_err()
                # **EXIT 1 IS THE PROGRAM BEING REFUSED, AND SINCE 2026-09-16
                # THAT IS A PASS** (defect 048, panel 157 R1 and R3). `--emit-c`
                # owes the artifact of a build that PASSED, so a program the
                # compiler rejects gets no C — which is the whole repair, and a
                # suite that called it a failure would be asserting the defect.
                #
                # **The condition is not a list of names**, which would be a
                # premise about the world and would expire in silence: it is
                # that NOTHING IS BLESSED for this program. A blessed file
                # standing beside a refused program is yesterday's artifact of
                # a program that should have none, and it stays a failure below.
                # So retiring the file is what records the decision, and the
                # decision cannot drift from the instrument.
                #
                # Exit 1 and exit 2 are not interchangeable here: 1 is the
                # input having diagnostics and 2 is the tool unable to run
                # (`.claude/rules/cli-surface.md`), so only the first is a
                # verdict about the author's program.
                report.keep_pass(@r)
            else
                blame: str @= "  " + one.source + " emits nothing: exit " + said.must().code.to_str() + "\n"

                if said.must().code == 2
                    blame @ blame + "  exit 2 is the compiler blaming ITSELF — it wrote C it could not compile\n"

                if said.must().code == 1
                    blame @ blame + "  exit 1 is the program REFUSED, which is legal since defect 048 — but " + one.stored + " still blesses an artifact it may no longer have; retire that file\n"
                why = absence.refused(lacks)

                if why.len() > 0
                    blame @ blame + "  not skipped for a missing library: " + why + "\n"
                report.keep_failure(@r, label: "emission/" + one.label, detail: blame + said.must().err)
            continue
        judge(@r, one: one, emitted: said.must().out, update: update)

        # The same fresh bytes, asked the descriptor invariant. This is
        # the only place in the harness holding a FRESH emission of every
        # program, so asking here costs one function call and catches a
        # pruning mistake at the moment it is made — the blessed files
        # cannot, because they are yesterday's answer. One rule, two
        # callers: `suite_descriptors` runs it over the committed bytes,
        # this runs it over the bytes just written, and neither owns a
        # copy of the scanner.
        suite_descriptors.judge(@r, label: "emission/" + one.label + " descriptors", c: said.must().out)

    if only == ""
        orphans(@r, scratch: scratch, store: store, seen: seen)

    # A skip is not a pass, asked of the programs walked, as `run` asks it of
    # a filter's cases.
    if skipped > 0 && skipped * 3 >= walked.len()
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "emission",
            detail: "  " + skipped.to_str() + " of " + walked.len().to_str() + " programs were skipped for a missing library — this machine is not comparing emissions"
        )
    return ok(r)

# One program's emission against its blessed bytes — or, under
# `UPDATE_EMISSION`, in place of them.
function judge(@r: report.Report, one: Program, emitted: str, update: bool)
    if update
        if !is_a_capture(emitted: emitted, of: one.source)
            report.keep_failure(
                @r,
                label: "emission/" + one.label,
                detail: "  refused to bless " + one.stored + ": the capture does not open with the emitter's header line for " + one.source + " — a blessing writes whatever stood on stdout, and the blessed files are the one instrument whose source of truth no longer exists"
            )
            return
        wrote = write_file(path: one.stored, text: emitted)

        if wrote.is_err()
            report.keep_failure(@r, label: "emission/" + one.label, detail: "  cannot write " + one.stored)
        else
            report.keep_pass(@r)
        return
    blessed = read_file(one.stored)

    if blessed.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "emission/" + one.label,
            detail: "  nothing blessed at " + one.stored + " — a new program's emission is blessed on purpose:\n    UPDATE_EMISSION=1 heroes run tests/harness/main.hero -- <compiler> emission " + one.label
        )
        return

    if blessed.must() != emitted
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "emission/" + one.label,
            detail: "  " + one.source + " no longer emits what it was blessed to emit — " + one.stored + "\n" + differences(blessed: blessed.must(), emitted: emitted)
        )
        return
    report.keep_pass(@r)

# **The blessed bytes must also COMPILE** (task 1234's first half, panel 231's
# R8). A byte comparison cannot see that the bytes do not compile: of 240
# blessed emissions measured on 2026-09-16, 28 failed `-fsyntax-only` while
# green here (`.claude/rules/diagnostics-and-goldens.md` § An instrument
# watches the world: the predicate beside the bytes). Re-measured on
# 2026-10-28, 692 of 705 pass and every one of the 13 others stops at a
# header this machine lacks, which is the machine's, told as a skip; and an
# emission naming Darwin's own C is asked on Darwin alone (`run_all`). The
# words are the fewest that decide whether C11 with the compiler's dialect
# parses and type-checks, `-std=gnu11 -fsigned-char`, its warnings off,
# since the compiler's own warning words are its build's to hold and a
# second copy of them here would drift; a group's `pkgconfig` is asked of
# `pkg-config`, as the compiler asks it, and the root of each package a fetch
# placed beside the program, and each folder a `path` entry of its
# catalogue names, is searched after the program's folder, as the compiler
# asks the one file (defects 2351 and 2357, `package_units.with_roots`), so a
# package's own header is never told as one this machine lacks. The link and
# the two routes are the generator's oracle 4, the task's other half. Its
# captures and listings go under the run's own `scratch`, which the harness
# removes as it ends: until defect 2305 they went under the net's own tests'
# directory, one left behind by every run of this suite.
function compiles(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, one: Program)
    words: [str] @= [
        "clang"
        "-std=gnu11"
        "-fsigned-char"
        "-w"
        "-fsyntax-only"
        "-I"
        "runtime"
        "-I"
        directory_of(one.source)
    ]

    for root in placed_roots(scratch: scratch, source: one.source)
        words @ words.push("-I").push(root)

    for flag in package_flags(scratch: scratch, source: one.source)
        words @ words.push(flag)

    words @ words.push(one.stored)
    ran = shell.attempted(scratch: scratch, program: "clang", words: words)

    if ran.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "emission/" + one.label + "/compiles",
            detail: "  clang could not be asked: " + shell.refusal(ran)
        )
        return

    if ran.must().code == 0
        report.keep_pass(@r)
        return
    lacking = lacked_header(ran.must().err)

    if lacking != ""
        report.keep_skip(
            @r,
            label: "emission/" + one.label + "/compiles",
            detail: "  this machine lacks `" + lacking + "`, which the program binds"
        )
        return
    report.keep_failure(
        @r,
        label: "emission/" + one.label + "/compiles",
        detail: "  " + one.stored + " is blessed and clang refuses it under `-fsyntax-only`:\n" + ran.must().err
    )

# The header a refusal says the machine lacks, or "" where it says none:
# clang's own words, `fatal error: '<name>' file not found`.
function lacked_header(err: str) -> str
    for row in strings.lines(err)
        if strings.contains(text: row, needle: "fatal error: '") && strings.contains(text: row, needle: "' file not found")
            after = strings.split_text(text: row, separator: "fatal error: '")[1]
            return strings.split_text(text: after, separator: "'")[0]

    return ""

function directory_of(path: str) -> str
    i: i64 @= path.len()

    while i > 0
        if path[i - 1] == '/'
            return path.slice(from: 0, to: i - 1)
        i @ i - 1

    return "."

# Each tree a fetch placed beside the program, `heroes.packages/<key>/`, in
# the order its folder lists them, then each folder a `path` entry of the
# program's catalogue names, from the program's folder (defect 2357); none
# where the program has neither.
function placed_roots(scratch: str, source: str) -> [str]
    none: [str] = []
    dir = directory_of(source)
    folder = dir + "/" + placed_trees.FOLDER
    out: [str] @= []

    if shell.is_directory(folder)
        out @ shell.list_directories(scratch: scratch, dir: folder).default(none)
    catalogue = read_file(dir + "/heroes.toml")

    if catalogue.is_err()
        return out

    for row in strings.lines(catalogue.must())
        line = strings.trimmed(row)

        if strings.starts_with(text: line, prefix: "path = \"")
            named = strings.split_text(text: line, separator: "\"")[1]

            if strings.starts_with(text: named, prefix: "/")
                out @ out.push(named)
            else
                out @ out.push(dir + "/" + named)

    return out

# The `pkg-config --cflags` of every `pkgconfig` a module beside the program
# names, its own directory being where its modules are; a package
# `pkg-config` does not know adds nothing, and the header it would have found
# is then the machine's lack.
function package_flags(scratch: str, source: str) -> [str]
    flags: [str] @= []
    dir = directory_of(source)
    listed = shell.list_entries(scratch: scratch, dir: dir)

    if listed.is_err()
        return flags

    for path in listed.must()
        if !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".hero")
            continue
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            continue

        for row in strings.lines(text.must())
            if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "extern ") && strings.contains(text: row, needle: " pkgconfig \"")
                name = strings.split_text(text: strings.split_text(text: row, separator: " pkgconfig \"")[1], separator: "\"")[0]
                asked = shell.attempted(
                    scratch: shell.own_scratch(),
                    program: "pkg-config",
                    words: ["pkg-config", "--cflags", "--", name]
                )

                if !asked.is_err() && asked.must().code == 0
                    for word in strings.split_text(text: strings.trimmed(asked.must().out), separator: " ")
                        if word != ""
                            flags @ flags.push(word)

    return flags

# A blessed file no program claims.
#
# This is the guard against the quietest failure of all: a case renamed,
# and its old emission still sitting there being compared to nothing. The
# harness reports it and does not delete it — removing bytes from the
# repository is the author's act, not an instrument's.
function orphans(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, store: str, seen: {str: str})
    listing = shell.list_entries(scratch: scratch, dir: store)

    if listing.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "emission/store", detail: "  cannot list " + store)
        return
    stray: [str] @= []

    for entry in listing.must()
        if strings.ends_with(text: entry, suffix: ".c") && seen[entry].is_err()
            stray @ stray.push("    " + entry)

    if stray.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "emission/store",
            detail: "  a blessed emission no program claims — a renamed or deleted program leaves one behind, and it is then bytes nobody compares:\n" + stray.join("\n") + "\n  delete it in the same commit as the program, or bless the program that should own it"
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# The first three differing lines, side by side with their numbers — enough
# to name the class without printing a megabyte of C.
function differences(blessed: str, emitted: str) -> str
    left = strings.lines(blessed)
    right = strings.lines(emitted)
    shown: [str] @= []
    i: i64 @= 0

    while i < left.len() && i < right.len() && shown.len() < 3
        if left[i] != right[i]
            shown @ shown.push(
                "  line " + (i + 1).to_str() + ":\n    blessed: " + left[i] + "\n    now:     " + right[i]
            )
        i @ i + 1

    if shown.len() == 0
        return "  every line they share is identical, and they are not the same length: " + blessed.len().to_str() + " bytes blessed, " + emitted.len().to_str() + " now"
    return shown.join("\n") + "\n  (" + left.len().to_str() + " lines blessed, " + right.len().to_str() + " now)"

# Whether a captured emission is THIS program's, by the one line every
# generated unit opens with: `/* Generated by heroes <version> from <source>.
# Do not edit. */` (emit/decls.hero). Asked before a blessing writes and never
# after: the blessed files have no other source of truth (the port that
# produced them is archived), so a wrong capture blessed once is wrong for
# good. The path is checked exactly — a capture of another program is the
# race this guards against (M-robustness-guards step 5).
function is_a_capture(emitted: str, of: str) -> bool
    rows = strings.lines(emitted)

    if rows.len() == 0
        return false
    return strings.starts_with(text: rows[0], prefix: "/* Generated by heroes ") && strings.ends_with(text: rows[0], suffix: " from " + of + ". Do not edit. */")

# A stand-in compiler that emits a fixed text on stdout, as
# `suite_golden.stub` does — the suites are tested against a stub rather
# than against the real binary, because a test that needed
# ./target/debug/heroes would die on the day this harness matters most.
function emitter(scratch: str, name: str, says: str) -> str?
    path = scratch + "/" + name
    write_file(path: path, text: "#!/bin/sh\nprintf '%s' " + inside_a_script(says) + "\nexit 0\n")?
    made = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", path])?

    if made.code != 0
        return fail(code: "cannot_run", msg: "cannot make " + path + " executable")
    return ok(path)

# A one-program store, ready to bless or to compare against.
function one_program_tree(scratch: str, name: str) -> Program?
    tree = scratch + "/" + name
    shell.remove_tree(tree)?
    shell.mkdir_all(tree)?
    write_file(path: tree + "/x.hero", text: "function main()\n    print(1)\n")?
    return ok(Program(source: tree + "/x.hero", stored: tree + "/x.c", label: "one/x"))

# A tree of the repository's shape under `root`, which ends in `/`, for
# `collected`: the four groups, `examples/` and the store, made empty, then
# each of `files`, a path under `root`, written empty, its folders made.
function planted(root: str, files: [str]) -> ()?
    shell.remove_tree(root)?

    for group in GROUPS
        shell.mkdir_all(root + "tests/golden/" + group)?

    shell.mkdir_all(root + EXAMPLES)?
    shell.mkdir_all(root + STORE)?

    for path in files
        shell.mkdir_all(directory_of(root + path))?
        write_file(path: root + path, text: "")?

    return ok()

# The labels of `programs`, in their order.
function labels_of(programs: [Program]) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= []

    for one in programs
        out @ out.push(one.label)

    return out

# The version the STUB writes into its header, and it is not the compiler's
# on purpose: these cases drive a shell script that prints fixed text
# (`emitter`, above) and never `./heroes`, and `is_a_capture` reads the
# header's shape, prefix and source path, not its number. The fixtures said
# `0.0.1` until 2026-09-07, when the real number moved for the first time
# (CLAUDE.md §14, release tags) and six copies of it here would have had to
# move with it. A number no release will ever print pins nothing.
constant STUB_VERSION: str
    "0.0.0-stub"

# The header line the stub opens with for the program at `of`, newline included.
function stub_header(of: str) -> str
    return "/* Generated by heroes " + STUB_VERSION + " from " + of + ". Do not edit. */\n"

# The one-program store's blessed bytes: the header, then a body.
function emitted_fixture() -> str
    return stub_header(of: "x.hero") + "int main(void) { return 0; }\n"

test "an emission that matches its blessed bytes passes, and a changed one fails by line"
    # POSIX is this case's subject, and this machine answers for itself
    # (can_run_scripts carries the measurement).
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    one = one_program_tree(scratch: scratch, name: "emission-match")
    assert !one.is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: one.must().stored, text: emitted_fixture()).is_err()
    programs: [Program] = [one.must()]

    same = emitter(scratch: scratch, name: "stub-emit-same.sh", says: emitted_fixture())
    assert !same.is_err()
    green = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: same.must(),
        store: scratch + "/emission-match",
        programs: programs,
        update: false,
        least: 1,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !green.is_err()

    # THREE, and the number is named so it explains itself when it moves:
    # `judge` (the bytes), `suite_descriptors.judge` (the descriptor
    # invariant over the same fresh emission) and `orphans` (no blessed
    # file without a program). It said 2 from the day
    # `suite_descriptors.judge` was added inside `sweep` at
    # M-bootstrap-archive until 2026-08-25, red the whole time, because
    # nothing ran this file — a self-test that counts every `keep_pass` in
    # a function breaks whenever a check is added to that function, and
    # this comment is the cheapest thing that turns that into one edit.
    assert green.must().passed == 3
    assert green.must().failed == 0

    # One byte of the second line different: the report must name the line
    # and show both sides, because after the archive there is no second
    # compiler to ask what changed.
    changed = emitter(
        scratch: scratch,
        name: "stub-emit-changed.sh",
        says: stub_header(of: "x.hero") + "int main(void) { return 1; }\n"
    )
    assert !changed.is_err()
    red = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: changed.must(),
        store: scratch + "/emission-match",
        programs: programs,
        update: false,
        least: 1,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !red.is_err()
    assert red.must().failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: red.must().failures[0], needle: "line 2:")
    assert strings.contains(text: red.must().failures[0], needle: "return 1;")
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "a program with nothing blessed says how to bless it, and blessing writes the bytes"
    # POSIX is this case's subject, and this machine answers for itself
    # (can_run_scripts carries the measurement).
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    one = one_program_tree(scratch: scratch, name: "emission-new")
    assert !one.is_err()
    programs: [Program] = [one.must()]

    # The stub's header names the program's own path: a blessing checks the
    # first line against `one.source` exactly (is_a_capture, below).
    proper = stub_header(of: one.must().source) + "int main(void) { return 0; }\n"
    stub = emitter(scratch: scratch, name: "stub-emit-new.sh", says: proper)
    assert !stub.is_err()

    # Floored at 0: the floor counts the emissions blessed (defect 341), and
    # before the blessing this tree has none.
    red = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: stub.must(),
        store: scratch + "/emission-new",
        programs: programs,
        update: false,
        least: 0,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !red.is_err()
    assert red.must().failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: red.must().failures[0], needle: "UPDATE_EMISSION=1")

    # Narrowed to the program by its label, so the blessing it asks for writes
    # that program's bytes and no other's (defect 2341).
    assert strings.contains(text: red.must().failures[0], needle: "-- <compiler> emission one/x")

    blessed = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: stub.must(),
        store: scratch + "/emission-new",
        programs: programs,
        update: true,
        least: 0,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !blessed.is_err()
    assert blessed.must().failed == 0
    assert read_file(one.must().stored).default("") == proper
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "a blessed file no program claims is reported, and so is a collision and a short list"
    # POSIX is this case's subject, and this machine answers for itself
    # (can_run_scripts carries the measurement).
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    one = one_program_tree(scratch: scratch, name: "emission-orphan")
    assert !one.is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: one.must().stored, text: emitted_fixture()).is_err()
    store = scratch + "/emission-orphan"
    assert !write_file(path: store + "/gone.c", text: "/* the emission of a program that was renamed */\n").is_err()
    stub = emitter(scratch: scratch, name: "stub-emit-orphan.sh", says: emitted_fixture())
    assert !stub.is_err()
    programs: [Program] = [one.must()]

    got = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: stub.must(),
        store: store,
        programs: programs,
        update: false,
        least: 1,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !got.is_err()
    assert got.must().failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: got.must().failures[0], needle: "gone.c")

    # Two programs, one blessed file: the second must be reported rather
    # than compared, and the floor must fire on a list below its count.
    twice: [Program] = [
        one.must()
        Program(source: one.must().source, stored: one.must().stored, label: "one/twin")
    ]
    clash = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: stub.must(),
        store: store,
        programs: twice,
        update: false,
        least: 3,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !clash.is_err()
    assert strings.contains(
        text: clash.must().failures[0],
        needle: "2 programs whose emission is blessed, and there were 3"
    )
    assert strings.contains(text: clash.must().failures[1], needle: "claimed by one/x as well")
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# DEFECT 341, held as a test (2026-10-07): the floor counted the programs it
# collected, so a program `--emit-c` refuses, with nothing blessed, counted as an
# emission compared. Two programs, one blessed and one refused: the refusal is
# read as one and passes, and a floor of two is NOT met, since one emission is
# blessed; until that day the floor read two programs and was met.
test "fixedbugs: the floor counts the emissions blessed, and a refusal is read as one and counted apart (defect 341)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    one = one_program_tree(scratch: scratch, name: "emission-341")
    assert !one.is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: one.must().stored, text: emitted_fixture()).is_err()
    tree = scratch + "/emission-341"
    assert !write_file(path: tree + "/refused.hero", text: "function main()\n    print(1)\n").is_err()
    refused = Program(source: tree + "/refused.hero", stored: tree + "/refused.c", label: "one/refused")
    path = scratch + "/stub-emit-341.sh"
    script = "#!/bin/sh\ncase \"$2\" in\n    *refused.hero) printf 'error[ffi_return_type]: refused\\n' 1>&2; exit 1 ;;\nesac\nprintf '%s' " + inside_a_script(emitted_fixture()) + "\nexit 0\n"
    assert !write_file(path: path, text: script).is_err()
    assert !shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", path]).is_err()
    programs: [Program] = [one.must(), refused]
    assert blessed_among(programs) == 1
    met = sweep(scratch: scratch, compiler: path, store: tree, programs: programs, update: false, least: 1, only: "")
    assert !met.is_err()

    # The blessed one twice (its bytes, its descriptors), the refusal once, and
    # the store: four, and nothing failed.
    assert met.must().passed == 4
    assert met.must().failed == 0
    short = sweep(scratch: scratch, compiler: path, store: tree, programs: programs, update: false, least: 2, only: "")
    assert !short.is_err()
    assert short.must().failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(
        text: short.must().failures[0],
        needle: "1 programs whose emission is blessed, and there were 2"
    )
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "two emissions of different lengths report the lengths rather than a line"
    grown = differences(blessed: "a\nb\n", emitted: "a\nb\nc\n")
    assert strings.contains(text: grown, needle: "not the same length")
    assert strings.contains(text: grown, needle: "4 bytes blessed")
    shifted = differences(blessed: "a\nb\n", emitted: "a\nz\n")
    assert strings.contains(text: shifted, needle: "line 2:")

# Quoting for the CONTENT of a shell script this test writes, which is
# not the same job `shell.sq` had. That one wrapped a filename on its way
# to a command line the harness was composing; this wraps a word inside a
# file that a shell will read later, on its own. The harness composes no
# command lines any more, so the function went with them — but a script
# is still a script, and its text still has to be well formed.
function inside_a_script(text: str) -> str
    out: str @= "'"

    for ch in text.chars()
        if ch == "'"
            out @ out + "'\\''"
        else
            out @ out + ch

    return out + "'"

test "a blessing refuses a capture that does not open with the emitter's header, and blesses one that does"
    # POSIX is this case's subject, and this machine answers for itself
    # (can_run_scripts carries the measurement).
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    one = one_program_tree(scratch: scratch, name: "emission-doctored")
    assert !one.is_err()
    programs: [Program] = [one.must()]

    # A capture with no header — what another run's stdout, or a wrong
    # compiler's, would leave: refused, and nothing is written.
    # Floored at 0 here and below: nothing is blessed before the blessing, and
    # the floor counts the emissions blessed (defect 341).
    wrong = emitter(scratch: scratch, name: "stub-emit-doctored.sh", says: "int main(void) { return 0; }\n")
    assert !wrong.is_err()
    refused = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: wrong.must(),
        store: scratch + "/emission-doctored",
        programs: programs,
        update: true,
        least: 0,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !refused.is_err()
    assert refused.must().failed == 1
    assert read_file(one.must().stored).is_err()

    # The same program's own header, naming its own path: blessed.
    proper = stub_header(of: one.must().source) + "int main(void) { return 0; }\n"
    right = emitter(scratch: scratch, name: "stub-emit-proper.sh", says: proper)
    assert !right.is_err()
    blessed = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: right.must(),
        store: scratch + "/emission-doctored",
        programs: programs,
        update: true,
        least: 0,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !blessed.is_err()
    assert blessed.must().failed == 0
    assert read_file(one.must().stored).must() == proper

    # A header naming ANOTHER program is the race itself, and is refused.
    assert !is_a_capture(emitted: stub_header(of: "other.hero"), of: "x.hero")
    assert is_a_capture(emitted: stub_header(of: "x.hero") + "#include", of: "x.hero")
    assert !is_a_capture(emitted: "", of: "x.hero")
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# DEFECT 246's cause in this suite, held as a test (2026-10-04): the skip took
# the compiler's words for the machine's. A header the machine has, said to be
# missing, is judged and fails; a header no machine has, which the program
# binds, is skipped by name, and alone it is a third of the sweep, which the
# floor refuses.
test "fixedbugs: a program that cannot emit is skipped only where the machine lacks what it binds"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    tree = scratch + "/emission-absence"
    assert !shell.remove_tree(tree).is_err()
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(tree + "/store-has").is_err()
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(tree + "/store-lacks").is_err()
    compiler = absence.said_stub(scratch)
    assert !compiler.is_err()
    has = absence.planted_case(dir: tree, name: "has", bound: "stdio.h", missing: "stdio.h")
    assert !has.is_err()

    # Blessed, so the rule for a refused program with nothing blessed is not
    # what answers: only the witness can.
    blessed = tree + "/store-has/has.c"
    assert !write_file(path: blessed, text: "int main(void) { return 0; }\n").is_err()
    refuted = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: compiler.must(),
        store: tree + "/store-has",
        programs: [Program(source: has.must(), stored: blessed, label: "has")],
        update: false,
        least: 1,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !refuted.is_err()
    assert refuted.must().failed == 1
    assert refuted.must().skips.len() == 0
    assert strings.contains(text: refuted.must().failures[0], needle: "clang finds `stdio.h`")
    lacks = absence.planted_case(dir: tree, name: "lacks", bound: absence.NOWHERE, missing: absence.NOWHERE)
    assert !lacks.is_err()

    # Blessed as well, as a program is that emits where its library is: the
    # floor of 1 counts it (defect 341), and only the skip answers here.
    assert !write_file(path: tree + "/store-lacks/lacks.c", text: "int main(void) { return 0; }\n").is_err()
    excused = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: compiler.must(),
        store: tree + "/store-lacks",
        programs: [Program(source: lacks.must(), stored: tree + "/store-lacks/lacks.c", label: "lacks")],
        update: false,
        least: 1,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !excused.is_err()
    assert excused.must().skips.len() == 1
    assert strings.starts_with(text: excused.must().skips[0], prefix: "SKIP emission/lacks\n")
    assert excused.must().failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: excused.must().failures[0], needle: "1 of 1 programs were skipped")
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# DEFECT 2341, held as a test (2026-11-08): `collect` took each group's `.hero`
# files and never a case that is a folder, so the two of `run/` had no blessed
# emission. Every group holds one here, a catalogue and a placed tree inside
# it and a folder of modules beside it, which is no program; and `examples/`
# holds a folder named `.case`, an example like any other, named by its
# folder, since `.case` names a golden case and not an example.
test "fixedbugs: a .case folder of every group is one program, its main.hero, and a folder of modules is none (defect 2341)"
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    root = scratch + "/emission-folders/"
    files: [str] @= [
        "tests/golden/run/pkg.case/main.expected"
        "examples/tool/main.hero"
        "examples/odd.case/main.hero"
    ]

    for group in GROUPS
        for path in ["/flat.hero", "/pkg.case/main.hero", "/pkg.case/heroes.toml", "/pkg.case/heroes.packages/text@1.0.0-k/words.hero", "/modules/helper.hero"]
            files @ files.push("tests/golden/" + group + path)

    assert !planted(root: root, files: files).is_err()
    got = collected(scratch: scratch, root: root)
    assert !got.is_err()
    want: [str] = [
        "emit/flat"
        "emit/pkg"
        "fixedbugs/flat"
        "fixedbugs/pkg"
        "ir/flat"
        "ir/pkg"
        "run/flat"
        "run/pkg"
        "examples/odd.case/main"
        "examples/tool/main"
    ]
    assert labels_of(got.must()) == want

    # Its source the file it compiles, its blessed file named by the folder.
    assert got.must()[7].source == root + "tests/golden/run/pkg.case/main.hero"
    assert got.must()[7].stored == root + STORE + "/run-pkg.c"
    assert got.must()[3].source == root + "tests/golden/fixedbugs/pkg.case/main.hero"
    assert got.must()[8].stored == root + STORE + "/examples-odd.case-main.c"
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# A folder named `.case` with no `main.hero` directly inside is a case that
# stopped running: the walk stops on it by name, whichever group holds it, and
# never passes it over (defect 2341).
test "fixedbugs: a .case folder holding no main.hero stops the walk by name, never skipped in silence (defect 2341)"
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    root = scratch + "/emission-hollow/"
    files: [str] = [
        "tests/golden/run/flat.hero"
        "tests/golden/fixedbugs/hollow.case/heroes.toml"
        "tests/golden/fixedbugs/hollow.case/inner/main.hero"
    ]
    assert !planted(root: root, files: files).is_err()

    said = match collected(scratch: scratch, root: root)
        .ok _  => "collected"
        .err e => e.msg

    assert said == "golden case " + root + "tests/golden/fixedbugs/hollow.case holds no main.hero, the file it compiles"
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# The guards hold for a folder program as for a flat one (defect 2341): its
# emission is compared and its file claimed, it counts toward the floor, its
# file is an orphan once the folder goes, and a flat case of its name beside
# it is a collision, two programs and one blessed file.
test "fixedbugs: a folder program is compared, counted, claimed and collides as a flat one does (defect 2341)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    root = scratch + "/emission-guards/"
    assert !planted(root: root, files: ["tests/golden/run/flat.hero", "tests/golden/run/pkg.case/main.hero"]).is_err()
    store = root + STORE

    for name in ["/run-flat.c", "/run-pkg.c"]
        assert !write_file(path: store + name, text: emitted_fixture()).is_err()

    stub = emitter(scratch: scratch, name: "stub-emit-guards.sh", says: emitted_fixture())
    assert !stub.is_err()
    two = collected(scratch: scratch, root: root)
    assert !two.is_err()
    assert blessed_among(two.must()) == 2
    green = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: stub.must(),
        store: store,
        programs: two.must(),
        update: false,
        least: 2,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !green.is_err()

    # Each program's bytes and descriptors, and the store: five.
    assert green.must().passed == 5
    assert green.must().failed == 0
    assert !shell.remove_tree(root + "tests/golden/run/pkg.case").is_err()
    one = collected(scratch: scratch, root: root)
    assert !one.is_err()
    gone = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: stub.must(),
        store: store,
        programs: one.must(),
        update: false,
        least: 1,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !gone.is_err()
    assert gone.must().failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: gone.must().failures[0], needle: store + "/run-pkg.c")
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(root + "tests/golden/run/pkg.case").is_err()

    for path in ["tests/golden/run/pkg.case/main.hero", "tests/golden/run/pkg.hero"]
        assert !write_file(path: root + path, text: "").is_err()

    three = collected(scratch: scratch, root: root)
    assert !three.is_err()
    assert labels_of(three.must()) == ["run/flat", "run/pkg", "run/pkg"]
    clash = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: stub.must(),
        store: store,
        programs: three.must(),
        update: false,
        least: 2,
        only: ""
    )
    assert !clash.is_err()
    assert clash.must().failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: clash.must().failures[0], needle: "is claimed by run/pkg as well")
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# The third word narrows the sweep to the programs whose label holds it, and a
# blessing under it writes theirs alone, which is how this repair's own two
# emissions were blessed (defect 2341). A narrowed run asks no floor and no
# orphan, so a floor past the tree and a file no program claims leave it
# green; and a word no label holds walks nothing, which `main` refuses as a
# run that tested nothing.
test "a narrowed sweep compares and blesses only the programs whose label holds the word (defect 2341)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    root = scratch + "/emission-narrowed/"
    assert !planted(root: root, files: ["tests/golden/run/flat.hero", "tests/golden/run/pkg.case/main.hero"]).is_err()
    store = root + STORE
    two = collected(scratch: scratch, root: root)
    assert !two.is_err()
    pkg = two.must()[1]
    assert pkg.label == "run/pkg"

    # The stub's header names the folder program, so a blessing of the flat one
    # would be refused as another program's capture, and fail.
    proper = stub_header(of: pkg.source) + "int main(void) { return 0; }\n"
    stub = emitter(scratch: scratch, name: "stub-emit-narrowed.sh", says: proper)
    assert !stub.is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: store + "/run-gone.c", text: proper).is_err()
    blessed = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: stub.must(),
        store: store,
        programs: two.must(),
        update: true,
        least: 9,
        only: "run/pkg"
    )
    assert !blessed.is_err()
    assert blessed.must().failed == 0
    assert read_file(pkg.stored).default("") == proper
    assert read_file(two.must()[0].stored).is_err()
    compared = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: stub.must(),
        store: store,
        programs: two.must(),
        update: false,
        least: 9,
        only: "pkg"
    )
    assert !compared.is_err()

    # Its bytes and its descriptors, and nothing else asked.
    assert compared.must().passed == 2
    assert compared.must().failed == 0
    nothing = sweep(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: stub.must(),
        store: store,
        programs: two.must(),
        update: false,
        least: 9,
        only: "zzz"
    )
    assert !nothing.is_err()
    assert nothing.must().passed + nothing.must().failed + nothing.must().skips.len() == 0
    assert narrowed(programs: two.must(), only: "").len() == 2
    assert narrowed(programs: two.must(), only: "run/").len() == 2
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# The blessed one file of a program that fetched a package includes the
# package's own header, which its build finds at the package's root: the
# compile here searches each placed tree, and each folder a `path` entry
# names (defect 2357), and a program that has neither searches none (defect
# 2351).
test "fixedbugs: the compile of a blessed one file searches each tree a fetch placed beside its program and each `path` folder (defects 2351 and 2357)"
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    root = scratch + "/emission-placed/"
    files = [
        "app/main.hero"
        "app/heroes.packages/a@1.0.0-k/a.hero"
        "app/heroes.packages/b@2.0.0-k/b.h"
        "app/local/loc.h"
        "lone/main.hero"
    ]
    assert !planted(root: root, files: files).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "app/heroes.toml", text: "[dependencies.loc]\npath = \"local\"\n").is_err()
    assert placed_roots(scratch: scratch, source: root + "app/main.hero") == [root + "app/heroes.packages/a@1.0.0-k", root + "app/heroes.packages/b@2.0.0-k", root + "app/local"]
    assert placed_roots(scratch: scratch, source: root + "lone/main.hero").len() == 0
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()
